I can replicate the canary issue with `strict`. If I run with `WIRESHARK_DEBUG_WMEM_OVERRIDE=simple` on an ASAN build, I get the following:

```
=================================================================
==457433==ERROR: AddressSanitizer: heap-buffer-overflow on address 0x7baba02d173c at pc 0x7f8ba85e4f2c bp 0x7ffd11c76130 sp 0x7ffd11c76128
READ of size 4 at 0x7baba02d173c thread T0
    #0 0x7f8ba85e4f2b in dissect_s7commp_ssl /home/johnthacker/wireshark/epan/dissectors/packet-s7comm_plus.c:11839:60
    #1 0x7f8baace6441 in dissector_try_heuristic /home/johnthacker/wireshark/epan/packet.c:3129:9
...

0x7baba02d173c is located 0 bytes after 12-byte region [0x7baba02d1730,0x7baba02d173c)
allocated by thread T0 here:
    #0 0x5566ad6a0368 in malloc (/home/johnthacker/wireshark-qt6-clang-build/run/wireshark+0xcd368) (BuildId: 595c7f70e5b7494754ff298af5836396b93fc8d5)
    #1 0x7f8ba5ef1be9 in g_malloc (/lib64/libglib-2.0.so.0+0x47be9) (BuildId: 0de4350a460a3b0e96af0edcd70c497f76377d3b)
    #2 0x7f8ba64ab41c in wmem_alloc /home/johnthacker/wireshark/wsutil/wmem/wmem_core.c:35:16
    #3 0x7f8ba64b4ec9 in wmem_simple_alloc /home/johnthacker/wireshark/wsutil/wmem/wmem_allocator_simple.c:43:50
    #4 0x7f8ba64ab577 in wmem_alloc /home/johnthacker/wireshark/wsutil/wmem/wmem_core.c:44:12
    #5 0x7f8ba85e8077 in dissect_s7commp /home/johnthacker/wireshark/epan/dissectors/packet-s7comm_plus.c:11594:42
```

packet-s7comm_plus.c:11594:
```c
                    conversation_state = wmem_new(wmem_file_scope(), conv_state_t); 
                    conversation_state->state = CONV_STATE_NEW;
                    conversation_state->start_frame = 0;
                    conversation_state->start_opcode = 0;
                    conversation_state->start_function = 0;
                    conversation_add_proto_data(conversation, proto_s7commp, conversation_state);
```

packet-s7comm_plus.c:11839:
```c
                    more_frags &= (ssl_conversation_state->remaining_len != DESEGMENT_ONE_MORE_SEGMENT);
```

where `ssl_conversation_state` was retrieved in line 11780:

```c
        ssl_conversation_state = (ssl_conv_state_t *)conversation_get_proto_data(conversation, proto_s7commp);
        if (ssl_conversation_state == NULL) {
            ssl_conversation_state = wmem_new(wmem_file_scope(), ssl_conv_state_t);
```

It appears that you have two different types of structs, `ssl_conv_state_t` and `conv_state_t`, either of which can be attached to a conversation for `proto_s7commp`. When the memory error occurs, what has happened is that you have retrieved what is actually a pointer to a `conv_state_t`, but you are casting it to a pointer to a `ssl_conv_state_t`.

```c
typedef struct {
    int state;
    uint32_t start_frame;
    uint8_t start_opcode;
    uint16_t start_function;
} conv_state_t;


typedef struct {
    int ssl_state;
    int reasm_state;
    uint32_t start_frame;
    int remaining_len;
} ssl_conv_state_t;
```

The `ssl_conv_state_t` is larger (with alignment, 16 bytes instead of 12 bytes), and the `remaining_len` occurs at the end. Therefore, when you attempt to access the `remaining_len` member you are overflowing the buffer that was actually allocated to the `conv_state_t` by treating it as a larger `ssl_conv_state_t`.

Part of it appears to be that `s7commp_decode_value_extended()` decodes past the end of the supposed `length_of_value` when handling multiple STAIs. I'm assuming `length_of_value` is in bytes, since its value appears to come from the `s7comm-plus.item.val.blobsize` field which is immediately followed by a `s7comm-plus.value.blob` field of precisely that many bytes, but just the list of "Lids" is longer than that, being four bytes per lid plus two bytes to hold the number of lids.

The problem can be made easier to see by creating a sub-TVB to pass to `s7commp_decode_value_extended()` so Wireshark's bounds checking catch it earlier. In `s7commp_decode_value()` replacing this:

```c
        /* Extended decoding of some known and interesting IDs */
        s7commp_decode_value_extended(tvb, pinfo, current_tree, value_start_offset, datatype, datatype_flags, sparsearray_key, length_of_value, id_number, relid, disable_vlq);
```

with this:

```c
        /* Extended decoding of some known and interesting IDs */
        tvbuff_t *ext_tvb = tvb_new_subset_length(tvb, value_start_offset, length_of_value);
        s7commp_decode_value_extended(ext_tvb, pinfo, current_tree, 0, datatype, datatype_flags, sparsearray_key, length_of_value, id_number, relid, disable_vlq);
```

causes the parsing of packet 110 to stop after the first `[HmiInfo]` field.

I think my dev environment is messed up though, because the malformed packet exceptions should be showing me more info on the console and they aren't.
